r foreshadowing the grand  line and how it will overshadow the east  blue  secondly the artwork here already has  quite decent framing and composition  the way the ship is later cut in two  with the bharati as a reference point  for its size  and the way that the ship's figurehead  seems to be in pain here  are wonderful details in the shots and  thirdly while i never shy away from  mentioning that there are other mangaka  out there that outperform oda in terms  of battle art and the overall battle  system  oda has a unique sense for creating  drama and conveying  feelings through body language and  dialogue in his scenes  [Applause]  they almost feel like conversations in  themselves  thus moments like mihawk stopping zaro's  three blades with his pocket knife  the powerful moment before he stabs  sorrow or mihawk  actually cutting zorro down all have  great composition  and the line of events is portrayed in a  way that builds emotion and stakes  very fast for the audience also i feel  like the updated version of the anime  does a particularly great job here  updating oda's work and giving it a more  modern and dynamic feel  okay but now let's finally look at the  dialogue in the scene which i think  brings all of these other elements  together  and truly makes the scene unique and  iconic  before we can get into the fantastic  conversation between zorro mihawk and  luffy  i quickly want to direct your attention  to this panel here however  when nami tells johnny and yosaku that  she and the straw hats will meet again  if they're truly linked by fate  i think this is a very powerful line by  nami here in a number of ways  because as we know luffy and co end up  meeting her again in the end alright  but they don't just randomly encounter  her because of chance or fate  but because of luffy's will to make her  part of the crew  and save her from a misery that we can  see when nami wishes to be free  with tears streaming down her face after  leaving  so it's really not about the straw hats  being pushed forward by  fate as many people seem to believe as  was for instance the case with naruto or  ichigo in the end  but i rather think it is oda's way of  saying very early on in the story  that luffy doesn't care about destiny  and fate but  that his own strong will allows him to  forge his own path ahead and  overcome fate that's why i found it so  important to include this part into my  analysis as well  and i think it also nicely translates  into the dialogue between mihawk and  zorro  because mihawk acknowledges zorro for  his will and determination to surpass  him and  not because he was always destined to  become the strongest  when zoro refuses to step back after  mihawk nearly pierces his heart  he tells mihawk that if he stepped back  right now  he would lose his dream and honor as a  swordsman and would rather die  zorro proves his strong will here to  achieve his dream and it is this will  that changes mihawk's perception of him  as he says here your strength is not  often seen in this world  clearly it's not soros physical strength  so for the very first time in this  moment i think  oda presents will or more specifically  strength of will  as the most important quality for his  characters that counts  way more than raw physical ability and  even more than  faith and ultimately with the  introduction of haki it becomes clear  that willpower literally surpasses  both of these things you can use your  willpower to drastically increase your  fighting power  and you can even use it to glimpse ahead  into the future  and change your own fate i'm honestly  not sure if oda  already had the concept of haki plan out  at this early point in the story  and if i had to bet i would probably say  no  but it is painfully obvious that will  was gonna be  the essential driving force in the story  right from the start  similarly mihawk also recognizes luffy's  will to strive to become pirate king  without a hint of the mocking he had for  zorro at the start  of course soros fight played an  important part here  but i think that luffy being a conqueror  hockey user  probably already radiated a strong sense  of will  and it is probably also at this moment  here that mihawk remembers the story  that shanks told him of how he lost his  arm  seeing luffy's powers and the straw hats  i think we can  even see this moment of recognition in  this panel here  where luffy first stretches his arm but  i think the dialogue does even more here  though  as especially in the japanese but also  in the english version  the words that mihawk uses to address  sorrow more and more reflect his change  in attitude  and his rising level of respect for him  his speech pattern switches from this  by his first name and challenges him to  surpass him  i think to some degree this is due to  mihawk yearning for a true challenge  after losing shanks  but i also believe that as a master  swordsman and sharing zoro's values and  beliefs  he has chosen zorro as worthy to follow  in his footsteps  and keep these values alive and defend  their craft against non-believers  if you want to bring it back to the  religious theme  and so i believe it makes for an even  starker contrast when mihawk is finally  approached by don creek again  as his face completely reverts from an  excited glow  to disgust and annoyance and he slashes  the ship once more  before disappearing because i feel with  this  he contrasts the difference between  luffy and creek before their battle even  begins  now last but not least i of course want  to address zoro's speech to luffy  which is the second of my favorite zorro  moments in the scene  after luffy has displayed true care and  fear for his first mate's life  even shedding a rare tear zaro now  publicly acknowledges and honors luffy  for the very first time in the story  zorro clearly has realized luffy's true  love and respect for him  as he apologizes for worrying his  captain he declares that he will never  lose again  and that he will stand by the next  pirate king making clear that he fully  believes in luffy and his dreams  just as luffy believes in him as far as  i can remember it's the only time we  ever see zorro cry in the story  outside of queen's death and it makes  the scene  just the more impactful for seeing the  otherwise stoic  and composed samurai this emotional and  vulnerable  i think it's really what makes this  scene even better when you come back to  it later  and so in just about three chapters  zorro has  established himself as the most  honorable loyal and determined person  that luffy could have ever wished for as  his right hand man  and the reasons why i love his character  so much  this scene serves as a basis for so  many other wonderful zaro moments in the  story and it's an important element for  making him a deeper and more complex  character  and thus distinguishes him from most  other strong characters  it's a moment that is incredibly  important for his and luffy's  development  and their fights are ultimately the  reason why sanji decides to join them  and follow his own dream as well  another moment with zorro i really liked  in the scene is when nami is leaving  zaro thinks it's a bad idea following  her and getting in trouble for it  but he follows his captain anyway this  is wonderfully referenced during the  whole cake island arc where he also is  skeptical about following sanji  but ends up respecting luffy's decision  anyway  as a result when the straw hats chase  after nami towards arlong park  we get another one of my favorite  moments in the story  luffy saving nami and giving her his hat  and so we'll be looking at that in the  next video so  stay tuned for that and don't forget to  leave me your feedback and ideas for the  next videos in the series  thanks guys peace


